you mind your work  and i will mind  mine rodrigo duterte was mayor of davao  city in the southern philippines for  more than 20 years he was catapulted  into the presidency by his ability to  convince millions of filipinos to rally  behind him  in office duterte quickly cemented his  role as an autocratic leader his war on  drugs was so violent that rights groups  believe at least 30 000 people have been  killed in police operations over the  last five years critics say he has used  state violence in ways not seen since  the martial law period under president  ferdinand marcus in the 1970s and 80s  duterte went after oligarchs but he also  jailed activists and members of the  opposition and shut down the country's  biggest media network and though he  promised that just a whiff of corruption  would be enough for him to fire  officials  years later most of those removed from  office have yet to face charges  he had to make deals with established  political forces so with that comes the  inability to sincerely push back against  institutionalized corruption the  president also hasn't revealed his own  wealth refusing to release an official  statement of assets liabilities and net  worth the first president to do so in  decades  when he was mayor he did not strengthen  he did not need a check and balance  because he was a king of davao city so  he didn't need to listen  to  a co-equal body the pandemic and the  recession it triggered are perhaps the  most challenging issues duterte has  faced but still many believe he deserves  credit for prioritizing large-scale  infrastructure projects  duterte made no secret of his admiration  for chinese president xi jinping and at  first he was so eager to be in china's  good graces that duterte cancelled a  defense agreement with the country's  strongest ally the united states a  decision he reversed just a few months  ago  as duterte enters his final days in  office he remains an enigma one of the  most polarizing but also one 