l now  police say lal received many threats for  his social media posts and had sought  police protection  his wife told local media that he had  skipped work for days before his killing  investigators say they're treating his  killing as a case of terrorism parvital  jazeera new delhi  let's go back to richard heydarian then  in manila richard will people in general  be sad to see duterte go are they better  off now  than they were when he came to power  well that's a difficult question i mean  if you look at it quantitatively meaning  the popularity ratings of duterte is  going out as the most popular filipino  president if not one of the most popular  leaders ever i mean close to 80 approval  rating so that means vast majority of  filipinos seem to be satisfied with him  at the same time you know i think a lot  of filipinos are also  recognizing the fact that the country is  not necessarily in good place you have  two years of pandemic  five quarters of recession and the thing  with duterte really is that he's a  master of performative populism he knows  how to entertain people he knows how to  inspire people or instill fear but the  reality is if you look at duterte's  presidency yes there have been some  gains in terms of peace process in  mindanao some infrastructure development  some sort of pushing for independence  from the united states but overall his  presidency has been a bonfire of  institutions he has been very good in  undermining democratic institutions or  liberalism in the philippines but it has  not been a very effective statesman in  terms of creating a new order or a new  constitutional order altogether i wanted  to ask you more about that you mentioned  that the state of the country and and  whether after six years of duterte it is  actually better off in terms of its of  its wealth uh the rule of law and as you  you mentioned there that the state of  its institutions  right i mean in terms of the economy of  course duterte can make an excuse 